media: sun50i-h713-hdmirx: hand Wce_SetWindow the full window, as stock does

The driver told the firmware that source and destination were both exactly
1920x1080 and never touched it again. That is not what stock sends. Its own
log, captured on the device and kept in re/ida/IDA_hy310/elog_after_hdmird.txt,
shows what UpdateWce gets handed:

    win_mgr  src_cfg     : [ 0, 0, 7680, 4320][ 0, 0, 7680, 4320]
    win_mgr  dst_cfg     : [ 0, 0, 7680, 4320][ 0, 0, 7680, 4320]
    win_mgr  capture_cfg : [ 0, 0,  720,  240][ 0, 0,  720,  240]
    win_mgr  display_cfg : [ 0, 0, 1920, 1080][ 0, 0, 1920, 1080]

7680x4320 is not a mode, it is the "no crop" sentinel. Stock leaves both
configurable windows wide open and lets the firmware map what it captured onto
what the panel is; the next lines of that same log show it doing exactly that
for a 720x480 source:

    wce_proc in_win_size : [  720,  480]
    wce_proc out_win_size: [ 1920, 1080]
    wce_proc ratio [24576 x 29127]

720 * 65536 / 1920 = 24576 and 480 * 65536 / 1080 = 29127 -- CalcScaleRatio
(sub_8B1A56B8) computing small over large in 16.16 fixed point. The scaler is
reached through this path and nowhere else.

Our 1920x1080 pinned both ends of that mapping together, and the hardware shows
it: with a 1280x720 source the capture block is rebuilt (INCAP 0x06940874 reads
0x050002D0 = 1280x720) while the scale ratio at 0x05180008 stays 0x43010000,
exactly 1:1, and 0x05180014 keeps bit 27, the firmware's "no scaling" flag. The
picture is then read at 1920 bytes per line out of 1280-byte lines, which is the
tearing on the wall.

Send the sentinel instead. This does not decide a geometry in the driver, it
stops the driver from deciding one -- the firmware measured the capture size and
drives the panel, so it is the one that knows both.

Measured on the device, and stated plainly: this alone does not fix the
tearing. With the sentinel in place, a 1280x720 source still leaves
0x05180008 at 0x43010000 and bit 27 of 0x05180014 set. What is missing is the
rebuild itself -- the register programming sits behind UpdateWce, which is
ticked by a hardware interrupt we cannot raise (re/notes/DEAD-ENDS.md section 81,
re-confirmed here: the full stock source-switch sequence -- Wce_SetWindow,
SetSource(1), SetSource(3), DisableBlackScreen -- runs clean and moves nothing).
Re-sending Wce_SetWindow after probe does nothing at all for the same reason.

The patch is kept because the value it replaces is wrong on its own terms: it
was a geometry the driver had no business choosing, it contradicts the only
capture we have of what stock sends, and it pins the two ends of the mapping
together -- so it would fight whatever eventually makes the rebuild run.

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 19:43:52.416235525 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 19:43:52.422128910 +0200
@@ -798,11 +798,20 @@
  * and hand back their addresses. The shared memory is mapped write combining;
  * it is written word by word because a wide unaligned access into this region
  * has faulted before (doku/67).
+ *
+ * The rectangle is the "no crop" sentinel, not a mode: stock puts
+ * [0, 0, 7680, 4320] into both windows and lets the firmware map the capture
+ * size it measured onto the panel size it drives. A real geometry here pins
+ * source and destination together, and the firmware then settles on 1:1 no
+ * matter what the source does.
  */
+#define H713_WCE_ANY_WIDTH	7680
+#define H713_WCE_ANY_HEIGHT	4320
+
 static void h713_hdmirx_wce_args(struct h713_hdmirx *rx, u32 *args)
 {
 	static const u32 window[4] = {
-		0, H713_HDMIRX_WIDTH, 0, H713_HDMIRX_HEIGHT,
+		0, H713_WCE_ANY_WIDTH, 0, H713_WCE_ANY_HEIGHT,
 	};
 	unsigned int i;
 
